Time complexity:
- Accessing an element by index: O(n) (because you have to traverse the list from the beginning or end)
- Inserting or deleting an element at the beginning or end: O(1) (because you just need to update a few references)
- Inserting or deleting an element in the middle: O(n) (because you have to traverse the list to find the position)
LinkedList is a good choice when you need to frequently add or remove elements from the list, especially at the beginning or end, and you don't need to access elements by index frequently.
 However, if you need fast access to elements by index, an ArrayList may be a better choice.

Deque (Double Ended Queue) is an interface in Java that extends the Queue interface and allows for the insertion and removal of elements at both ends of the queue.
LinkedList implements the Deque interface, which means it can be used as a double-ended queue.
Deque is useful when you need to add or remove elements from both ends of the queue, such as in a breadth-first search algorithm or when implementing a stack using a queue.

